WB.L("psychological-safety", "business", "intermediate", "Psychological safety: why the best teams admit mistakes",
  "A researcher expected the best hospital teams to make the fewest errors. Her data said the opposite, and that surprise changed how we think about teamwork.",
  [
    ["A puzzling result", "Amy Edmondson found that better hospital teams reported more errors, because they felt safe to speak up.",
      "In the 1990s researcher Amy Edmondson studied medication errors in hospital teams. She expected teams with better leadership and stronger relationships to make fewer mistakes. The data seemed to show the reverse: the better teams reported more errors.\n\nThen she realised what might be happening. The best teams were probably not making more mistakes. They were more willing to talk about them, while in teams where people feared blame, errors were hidden. That insight led to psychological safety, which Edmondson, later a Harvard Business School professor, defines as a shared belief in a team that it is safe to take interpersonal risks, such as asking a question, admitting a mistake or raising a concern."],
    ["Google's Project Aristotle", "Google found that how a team works together mattered more than who was on it, and safety mattered most.",
      "In 2012 Google launched Project Aristotle to find out what made some of its teams more effective than others. Researchers studied around 180 teams, expecting the right mix of skills, personalities or backgrounds to explain success. Those factors mattered far less than expected. What mattered most was how team members interacted.\n\nWhen Google published its findings in 2015, it listed five key dynamics: psychological safety, dependability, structure and clarity, meaning, and impact. Psychological safety was described as by far the most important, the foundation the others depended on. The findings drew wide attention and brought Edmondson's work to a far larger audience."],
    ["Not about being nice", "Psychological safety works best when it is combined with high standards.",
      "A common misunderstanding is that psychological safety means lowering expectations or avoiding criticism. Edmondson is clear that it does not. It also does not mean everyone agrees. It describes the climate of a group, not one person's confidence: the same person might speak freely in one team and stay silent in another. It means honest disagreement and bad news surface early, while there is still time to act.\n\nShe describes a grid. Low safety and low standards produce apathy. High safety with low standards is comfortable but unstretching. High standards with low safety produces anxiety, and people hide problems. The goal is high standards with high safety, her learning zone. Candour and kindness are not opposites."],
    ["Why silence is dangerous", "Fear does not stop problems; it mostly stops leaders hearing about them until they are bigger.",
      "The cost of silence can be enormous. Investigations into accidents in aviation, healthcare and engineering have often found that someone saw a warning sign but hesitated to raise it with a more senior person. From around the 1980s aviation responded with crew resource management training, which teaches junior crew to voice concerns clearly and captains to invite them.\n\nIn business the costs are usually quieter but still real: a flawed product nobody challenged, a project everyone privately doubted, a safety shortcut nobody reported. Fear does not stop problems happening. It mostly stops leaders hearing about them until they are much bigger and more expensive to fix."],
    ["Building it in your team", "Leaders build psychological safety by inviting input, admitting their limits and responding well to bad news.",
      "Psychological safety can be built, and leaders have the biggest influence. Edmondson suggests three broad practices. First, frame the work as a learning problem rather than simple execution, being honest that there is uncertainty and that everyone's input is needed. Second, acknowledge your own fallibility, with something as simple as saying you might be missing something. Third, model curiosity by asking genuine questions.\n\nJust as important is how you react when someone speaks up. Thanking a person for raising a problem, even unwelcome news, encourages others to do the same. Reacting with anger teaches the whole team to stay quiet. Trust is built slowly and can be damaged very quickly."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did the better hospital teams report more errors?", ["They made far more mistakes than other teams","They were more willing to talk about mistakes","They had fewer staff checking the work","They were required to report everything by law"], 1, "Teams without fear of blame surface errors, while fearful teams hide them."],
    [1, "What did Google's Project Aristotle find mattered most for team effectiveness?", ["The mix of skills and personalities on the team","The seniority of the team's manager","The size of the team's annual budget","How members interacted, led by psychological safety"], 3, "Team dynamics mattered more than who was on the team, with safety as the foundation."],
    [2, "How does Edmondson's 'learning zone' combine safety and standards?", ["Low standards with high safety","High standards with low safety","High standards with high safety","Low standards with low safety"], 2, "People are pushed to excel and feel able to say when something is going wrong."],
    [3, "What is the main danger of a team where people stay silent?", ["Leaders hear about problems only once they have grown","Meetings become much longer than planned","Teams disagree too often to make decisions","Staff stop working to high standards deliberately"], 0, "Silence does not prevent problems; it delays the warning that could prevent them."],
    [4, "How should a leader respond when someone raises unwelcome news?", ["Ask who else knew about it before the meeting","Thank them, so others feel safe to speak up too","Move on quickly without comment","Show disappointment so the mistake is not repeated"], 1, "How leaders react teaches the whole team whether speaking up is safe."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"The best hospital teams reported the most errors. Why would that be good?"});

WB.L("unit-economics", "business", "intermediate", "Unit economics: does each sale actually make money?",
  "A business can grow fast, win millions of customers and still be heading for ruin. Unit economics is the simple maths that tells you which kind of business you have.",
  [
    ["Profit on one sale", "Unit economics asks whether one sale makes money after its variable costs, before fixed costs are covered.",
      "Company accounts can hide a lot. Revenue may soar while losses grow faster. Unit economics asks a simple question: looking at one unit, such as a cup of coffee, a delivery, a subscription or a customer, does the business make or lose money? If each unit loses money, growth just loses money faster.\n\nImagine a coffee cart selling coffee at five dollars a cup. Beans, milk, cup and lid cost about one dollar seventy, and card fees add ten cents. These variable costs rise with every cup. What is left, roughly three dollars twenty, is the contribution margin, the amount each sale contributes to fixed costs and profit. If fixed costs such as permits, insurance and the owner's wage are 800 dollars a week, the cart must sell about 250 cups to break even."],
    ["The cost of winning customers", "Customer acquisition cost is everything you spend to gain one new customer, and it tends to rise as you grow.",
      "For many businesses, especially subscriptions and online services, the biggest cost is not making the product but finding the customer. Customer acquisition cost, or CAC, is the total spent on marketing and sales in a period divided by the new customers gained. If a business spends 10,000 dollars on advertising and wins 200 customers, its CAC is 50 dollars each.\n\nThe number is easy to underestimate. Free trials, discounts, sales staff wages and referral rewards all count. CAC also tends to rise as a business grows, because the easiest and most enthusiastic customers are won first, and later ones take more effort and money to persuade."],
    ["What a customer is worth", "Customer lifetime value estimates the profit, not revenue, a customer brings over the whole relationship.",
      "The partner to CAC is customer lifetime value, or LTV. It estimates how much profit, not just revenue, a typical customer generates over the whole time they stay. A simple version multiplies the margin per period by how many periods customers usually remain. A gym member who pays 20 dollars a week, of which 12 dollars is margin, and stays about a year on average, is worth roughly 600 dollars in margin.\n\nInvestors often compare the two numbers. A common rule of thumb says LTV should be around three times CAC or more, and that the acquisition cost should be earned back within a reasonable time. It is a guide, not a law."],
    ["The MoviePass warning", "MoviePass grew rapidly by paying cinemas more per customer than it charged them, and collapsed.",
      "MoviePass is one of the best-known examples of broken unit economics. In 2017 the American service cut its subscription to about ten US dollars a month, letting members see up to one movie in a cinema every day. The catch was that MoviePass paid cinemas close to the full ticket price for each film. A single ticket could cost more than a month's subscription.\n\nCustomers loved it, and membership soared into the millions within a year. But every enthusiastic moviegoer deepened the losses. The company tried restrictions, price changes and limits, which angered customers. The service shut down in 2019, and its parent company filed for bankruptcy in early 2020."],
    ["Asking the right questions", "Strong unit economics turn growth into an advantage instead of a threat.",
      "Unit economics is useful whether you run a market stall or assess a startup. The key questions are simple. What is the price, and what are the true variable costs of each sale? How much does it cost to win a customer, and how long do they stay? How long until a new customer pays back what it cost to win them?\n\nSome businesses deliberately lose money on early customers, expecting costs to fall with scale or customers to buy more later. Occasionally that works. Often it does not, and the plan rests on hope rather than evidence. The healthiest businesses can show that each unit makes money now, so every extra sale makes the company stronger."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "In the coffee cart example, what is the contribution margin?", ["5 dollars, the full price of each cup","About 1 dollar 80, the variable cost per cup","About 3 dollars 20, the price less variable costs","800 dollars, the weekly fixed cost"], 2, "Price minus variable costs is what each sale contributes towards fixed costs and profit."],
    [1, "Why does customer acquisition cost often rise as a business grows?", ["Advertising becomes cheaper per customer over time","Existing customers stop paying their bills","Staff wages fall as a business gets bigger","The easiest, most enthusiastic customers are won first"], 3, "Later customers take more effort and money to persuade."],
    [2, "What does customer lifetime value measure?", ["Profit from a customer over the whole relationship","Revenue from a customer's very first purchase","What it costs to win one new customer","The total number of customers a business has"], 0, "LTV counts profit over time, which can then be compared with what it cost to win the customer."],
    [3, "Why did MoviePass lose money as it grew?", ["It charged members too much for tickets","It paid cinemas more per film than members paid","It had too few members to cover its costs","It spent too little on advertising"], 1, "With negative unit economics, each extra enthusiastic customer deepened the losses."],
    [4, "What do healthy unit economics let a company do?", ["Skip fixed costs such as rent and wages","Rely on future customers to cover losses","Grow so each extra sale makes it stronger","Stop tracking how long customers stay"], 2, "When each unit makes money now, growth is an advantage rather than a threat."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A business can win millions of customers and still head for ruin. Here's the maths."});

WB.L("tupperware-brownie-wise", "business", "beginner", "Tupperware, Brownie Wise and the home party",
  "A clever plastic container was gathering dust on shop shelves. Then a single mother from Detroit worked out how to sell it, and changed direct selling for good.",
  [
    ["Great product, poor sales", "Tupperware's clever airtight seal sold poorly in shops because customers did not understand it.",
      "Earl Tupper, an American inventor from New Hampshire, worked for DuPont in the late 1930s and became interested in polyethylene, a new plastic. He developed ways to purify and mould it into containers that were light, unbreakable and flexible, unlike the brittle plastics of the time. His real breakthrough was the lid. Inspired by paint tin lids, he designed a seal that snapped on and could be made airtight by pressing out a little air, the famous burp. Tupperware went on sale after the Second World War.\n\nBut in hardware and department stores it sat on shelves. Plastic was a novelty, and the seal was not obvious from a container on a shelf. Many people could not work out how to close the lids. The product needed to be shown."],
    ["Enter Brownie Wise", "Brownie Wise discovered that Tupperware sold brilliantly when shown at home parties, and Tupper put her in charge.",
      "Some saleswomen working for home-selling companies such as Stanley Home Products had begun demonstrating Tupperware at gatherings in people's homes, with far better results than shops. One was Brownie Wise. Born in Georgia, she was a divorced single mother raising her son in Detroit. She found Tupperware sold brilliantly when women could see it, touch it and watch the lid seal.\n\nWise built a network of dealers and moved to Florida, and her group's results caught Tupper's attention. In 1951 he pulled Tupperware out of shops altogether and committed to home parties. He put Wise in charge of the new sales operation, Tupperware Home Parties, with the title of vice-president."],
    ["How a party works", "The home party combined demonstration, social connection and the trust of friends.",
      "The home party model was simple but powerful. A host invited friends and neighbours to her house. A dealer demonstrated the products, often with games and a playful show of how the seal kept food fresh or how a sealed bowl full of liquid could be tossed without spilling. Guests could handle everything and place orders, and the host received a gift for organising it.\n\nBuying from a friend in a relaxed social setting created a sense of trust and obligation that a shop counter could not match. For many women in the 1950s, who were largely excluded from business careers, becoming a dealer offered independent income, flexible hours and the chance to rise through a sales organisation."],
    ["Recognition and a fall", "Wise built a culture of celebration and became a business celebrity, then was dismissed in 1958.",
      "Wise understood motivation. At the Florida headquarters she held lavish annual events, called jubilees, where top sellers won prizes ranging from appliances to cars and boats. Recognition mattered as much as money. Sales grew rapidly, and in 1954 Wise became the first woman on the cover of the American magazine Business Week.\n\nBut her fame and independence led to clashes with Tupper, who reportedly resented her growing profile. In 1958 he dismissed her. That same year he sold the company to the Rexall Drug Company for about sixteen million US dollars. Wise received a modest payout, and for years her role was largely left out of the company's official history."],
    ["The party plan's legacy", "The party plan spread to many products, and its core lesson is that a product customers do not understand will not sell.",
      "Tupperware became a global brand, and the party plan was copied by companies selling cosmetics, kitchenware, candles, jewellery and more. It works because it combines demonstration with personal relationships, though critics note that social pressure to buy from a friend can be uncomfortable.\n\nTimes changed. As more women worked outside the home and shopping moved online, the party model struggled, and Tupperware's sales declined for years. In 2024 the company filed for bankruptcy protection in the United States. Yet the core lesson remains sharp. A product that customers do not understand will not sell, however good it is. Finding the right way to show it can matter as much as inventing it."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did Tupperware sell poorly in shops?", ["The containers were too expensive for most people","Shoppers could not see how the airtight seal worked","The plastic cracked easily in transport","Shops refused to stock plastic goods"], 1, "The product needed to be shown and handled, which a shelf could not do."],
    [1, "What did Brownie Wise discover about selling Tupperware?", ["It sold best when heavily discounted in shops","It sold only to people who already owned it","It needed television advertising to succeed","It sold well when people could see and handle it"], 3, "Seeing and touching the product, and watching the lid seal, made the difference."],
    [2, "Why did the home party model work so well?", ["Demonstrations plus the trust of friends","Prices well below what any shop charged","Pressure on every guest to buy something","Free home delivery for every guest's order"], 0, "It combined hands-on demonstration with personal trust in a social setting."],
    [3, "How did Wise's career end at Tupperware?", ["She retired after the company was sold","She was promoted to run the whole company","Tupper dismissed her in 1958 after a clash","She resigned to start a rival company"], 2, "Her fame and independence clashed with Tupper, and her contribution was long overlooked."],
    [4, "What is the core lesson of the Tupperware story?", ["A product people do not understand will not sell","Plastic products always sell better than others","Home parties remain the best way to sell anything","Low prices matter more than clear demonstrations"], 0, "Finding the right way to show a product can matter as much as inventing it."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A brilliant container sat unsold until one woman showed people how to use it."});

WB.L("valuing-a-business", "business", "advanced", "What is a business worth? Valuation explained",
  "Two experts can look at the same company and arrive at prices billions apart. Valuation is part maths, part judgement, and understanding it changes how you see every price tag in the market.",
  [
    ["Price versus value", "The price of a business is what someone pays; its value is an estimate of the cash it can produce for its owners.",
      "Price is what you pay; value is what you get. Warren Buffett, a student of the investor Benjamin Graham, put it that way. A price is a fact, such as the latest share price or an agreed sale. Value is an estimate of what the business is really worth to its owners, and estimates differ. A buyer and a seller can disagree sharply, both in good faith.\n\nMost serious valuation rests on one idea: a business is worth the cash it will generate for its owners over its remaining life. Everything else, from complex spreadsheets to rules of thumb, is a way of estimating that cash and what it is worth today."],
    ["Money today beats tomorrow", "A dollar in the future is worth less than a dollar now, so analysts discount future cash back to the present.",
      "Would you rather have 100 dollars today or in a year? Almost everyone says today. Money in hand can be invested, it avoids the risk of a broken promise, and inflation can erode what future money buys. So analysts discount future amounts back to the present. If you could earn 10 per cent elsewhere, 100 dollars in a year is worth about 90.91 dollars today, because that sum grows to 100 at 10 per cent.\n\nThis is the base of discounted cash flow (DCF). An analyst forecasts the free cash flow in each future year, meaning cash left after running the business and making necessary investments. Each year is discounted to today's dollars, then the results are added up."],
    ["Discount rate and risk", "Riskier cash flows are discounted at higher rates, and small changes in the rate can swing a valuation sharply.",
      "Nobody can forecast forever, so analysts model five or ten years in detail, then add one terminal value for everything after. A common method treats the business as growing steadily for ever: next year's cash flow divided by the discount rate minus the growth rate. Often this is most of the total.\n\nThe discount rate is where judgement and risk enter. It is the return investors require for backing this business instead of another. A stable utility earns a lower rate than an uncertain young technology firm. Small changes matter. One million dollars of cash flow growing 2 per cent for ever is worth 12.5 million at a 10 per cent rate, but about 14.3 million at 9 per cent."],
    ["Comparison and debt", "Multiples value a business by comparing it with similar ones, and equity value is what remains for shareholders after debt.",
      "DCF is rigorous but laborious, so analysts also use multiples, much like valuing a house from recent sales nearby. The price to earnings ratio compares share price with profit per share. Enterprise value to EBITDA compares the whole business, debt included, with earnings before interest, tax, depreciation and amortisation. If similar firms trade at ten times EBITDA, a business earning five million might be worth about fifty million. Multiples are quick and market-based, but if the whole market is overpriced they copy the error.\n\nEnterprise value is the whole operating business. Equity value is roughly that minus net debt (debt less spare cash). An 800,000 dollar house with a 500,000 dollar mortgage gives you only 300,000 of equity."],
    ["Garbage in, garbage out", "A valuation is only as good as its assumptions, so test them and use a range rather than trusting one number.",
      "Valuation models look precise, with numbers to the last dollar, but they rest on forecasts of growth, margins and risk that nobody can know. As the example showed, a one percentage point change in the discount rate can shift a valuation by more than ten per cent. The long-term growth rate has a similar effect.\n\nCareful analysts therefore present a range, test pessimistic and optimistic scenarios, and check the answer against multiples. Many experienced investors also want a margin of safety, another idea linked to Graham: buy only when the price is comfortably below their estimate of value, so that errors in their assumptions do not ruin them."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "According to most serious valuation, what is a business worth?", ["The average price of its recent share trades","The cash it will produce for its owners over time","The total cost of its buildings and equipment","Whatever the seller originally paid for it"], 1, "Valuation estimates the cash a business will produce for its owners and what that cash is worth today."],
    [1, "Why is 100 dollars received in a year worth less than 100 dollars today?", ["Banks refuse to hold money for longer than a year","Prices always fall over the following twelve months","Tax is charged only on money received later","Money now can be invested and avoids risk and inflation"], 3, "Return, risk and inflation all make money in hand more valuable, so future cash is discounted."],
    [2, "What happens to value when a higher discount rate is applied to the same cash flows?", ["It rises, because the cash looks more certain","It stays the same, because the cash flows have not changed","It falls, because riskier cash is worth less today","It doubles, because the future is weighted more heavily"], 2, "A higher required return shrinks the present value of future cash, so risky businesses are worth less today."],
    [3, "Why can two businesses with identical operations have different equity values?", ["One may carry more debt, leaving less for shareholders","Equity value ignores the debt each business carries","Enterprise value is always lower than equity value","Equity value is set by each company's revenue alone"], 0, "Equity value is roughly enterprise value minus net debt, so heavy borrowing leaves less for owners."],
    [4, "Why do careful analysts present a range of values instead of a single figure?", ["Regulators require analysts to give a range","Forecasts are uncertain and small changes matter","A range makes the price easier to negotiate down","A single figure suits only private businesses"], 1, "Because small assumption changes move the answer a lot, testing scenarios is more honest than one precise number."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Two experts can value the same company billions apart. Here's how."});

